🧠 Introduction

To write correct Java programs, you must understand:

✔ Java syntax rules
✔ Identifiers (naming rules)
✔ Data types (types of data stored)

👉 These are **fundamental concepts for coding & interviews 🔥**

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔤 1. Java Syntax

Syntax defines the **rules to write Java code correctly**.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Basic Syntax Rules

✔ Java is **case-sensitive**
✔ Every statement ends with `;`
✔ Code is written inside `{ }`
✔ File name = Class name

---

📌 Example

```java id="syn1"
public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Java Syntax Example");
    }
}
```

---

⚠️ Common Syntax Errors

❌ Missing semicolon `;`
❌ Incorrect brackets `{ }`
❌ Wrong class/file name
❌ Case mismatch

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔠 2. Identifiers in Java

Identifiers are **names given to variables, classes, methods, etc.**

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📌 Rules for Identifiers

✔ Must start with letter, `_`, or `$`
✔ Cannot start with number
✔ Cannot use keywords
✔ Case-sensitive

---

📌 Valid Examples

```java id="id1"
int age;
String name;
double salary;
```

---

❌ Invalid Examples

```java id="id2"
int 123age;     // starts with number
int class;      // keyword
int my-name;    // invalid character
```

---

💡 Naming Conventions (Best Practice)

✔ Class → PascalCase (`StudentData`)
✔ Variable → camelCase (`studentName`)
✔ Constant → UPPER_CASE (`MAX_VALUE`)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

🔢 3. Data Types in Java

Data types define **what type of data a variable can store**.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📊 Types of Data Types

🔹 1. Primitive Data Types

👉 Basic built-in types

| Type    | Size    | Example    |
| ------- | ------- | ---------- |
| int     | 4 bytes | 10         |
| float   | 4 bytes | 3.14f      |
| double  | 8 bytes | 3.14159    |
| char    | 2 bytes | 'A'        |
| boolean | 1 bit   | true/false |
| byte    | 1 byte  | 100        |
| short   | 2 bytes | 200        |
| long    | 8 bytes | 100000L    |

---

🔹 2. Non-Primitive Data Types

👉 Reference types

* String
* Arrays
* Classes
* Objects

---

📌 Example

```java id="dt1"
int age = 20;
double price = 99.99;
char grade = 'A';
boolean isPassed = true;
```

---

🎯 Why Data Types Matter?

✔ Define memory usage
✔ Ensure correct operations
✔ Improve performance

---

⚠️ Common Mistakes

❌ Using wrong data type
❌ Forgetting suffix (e.g., `f`, `L`)
❌ Type mismatch

---

🧠 Real Insight

👉 Choosing correct data type:

* Saves memory
* Improves efficiency
* Prevents bugs

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

📚 Key Takeaways

✔ Syntax defines structure of Java code
✔ Identifiers follow naming rules
✔ Data types define type of data
✔ Primitive & Non-primitive types exist

